# Are These People Criminals?
Tamil title: இவர்கள் குற்றவாளிகளா?

It was about eleven at night. Carrying sweet boiled milk in a silver vessel, Kokilam walked slowly towards the bedroom.

Seeing her approach, Amuthan lay as though exhausted and fast asleep.

"Here, drink the milk," she said, holding it out. Did the words enter his ears?

One can wake a person who is truly asleep. How can one wake someone who has deliberately chosen to sleep?

Kokilam recognized his pretence.

"Very well. One should not wake a sleeping man. I too shall go and sleep," she said, taking two steps away.

Amuthan sprang up, caught the loose end of her sari, and asked, "Where are you going?"

She laughed. Amuthan was bewitched by that laughter.

"Do you not want the milk?" Kokilam asked in a coaxing voice.

"I do not like it. You drink it," Amuthan replied tenderly.

"You do not like milk, and you do not like fruit. What do you like?"

"There is one thing I like."

"What is it? Only if you tell me can I bring you that at least."

"Bring what?"

"Your face close to mine."

"Why?"

"Because the thing I desire is there."

Hearing this, her joined lips parted slightly, revealing a bright row of teeth like a string of pearls. Then eye met eye with the feeling of love.

She drank the milk. Amuthan drank nectar from her lips.

Which was sweeter, the white milk or the red lips? They alone must answer. Only they knew the taste.

A man had been watching these events through the street-facing window. He was about forty years old, and his heart boiled. His arms and legs trembled.

Yet as time passed, his anger too began to pass. Consequently, his settled intention began to crumble. He hesitated in a world of thought.

One crucial matter confused his mind. From it, as in a moving picture, many questions appeared one after another in his thoughts. His brain seemed battered by a great cyclone.

After a little time, his struggling heart gained new life. His hesitant thought became clear. He appeared to have reached a decision, and a slight happiness came over his face.

But in the very next moment he was caught in another tremendous cyclone. He knew he could not escape it. Weariness overcame him. He weakened, and many drops fell from his eyes.

What had plunged him into so much thought and confusion? A single figure seen through the window. Its former appearance had been one thing; its present appearance was another. How could the sight of such a transformation fail to disturb his mind?

Even the clarity that came to his troubled brain was of no use. The fierce storm disturbed it again. The happiness that had begun to bud set at once. Exhausted, he leaned against the wall. Without his awareness, the object in his hand fell to the ground with a clang.

The sound interrupted the lovers' play inside the room. Amuthan immediately approached the window and looked into the street. In the moonlight, bright as broad daylight, no one could be seen. Then he looked along the side of the window and saw someone leaning against the wall.

"Who is there?" he called.

There was no answer. Amuthan took a battery torch, opened the street door, and stepped outside. Kokilam accompanied him.

By the light of the torch, Amuthan saw the man. His heart trembled. Kokilam too began to shake. Neither knew what to do, and they stood stunned.

There was neither anger nor cruelty upon the man's face. They saw that he suffered from a weary heart. Then they noticed a billhook lying on the ground, and their fear increased. Their bodies trembled. Yet the expression on his face seemed to tell them, "Do not fear."

Resolved to accept whatever might happen, Amuthan bowed to him.

"We wished to attain the human ideal. If that is a crime, punish us," he said.

Kokilam fell at the man's feet.

"Father, I betrayed you. I destroyed the honour of our lineage and brought ruin upon the family. It was not my crime. What power did I possess before the force of nature? Forgive me! Forgive me!" she pleaded, washing his feet with her tears.

This softened his heart still further. Sorrow overwhelmed him. He could not speak, nor could he remain there any longer. Wiping the overflowing tears from his eyes with his finger, he hurried down the street and disappeared.

You will have understood that the man who left was Kokilam's father.

Kokilam was a young widow, seventeen years old, a maiden with the gleaming beauty of youth and a body like a fresh green shoot. Humans endowed with six senses might accept that, at such a young age, she should suppress the feelings of pleasure arising within her, wear the inauspicious garb of widowhood, and remain disfigured. Would nature accept it?

Nature entrusted her to Amuthan. Amuthan brought away the treasure that had come to him, that fine and beautiful woman. He delighted in a happy life and enabled her to delight in it too.

When her parents learned that she had gone with Amuthan, they became enraged by the townspeople's slander and abuse. They hastened to erase the shame. That was why her father had come searching for her with a billhook in his hand.

Why did the intention with which he came fail?

When the would-be killer looked through the window, he began to doubt whether the woman inside was truly his daughter Kokilam. How could he not?

When she lived with them, her body had been covered in dust and wasted by grief. Her hair hung loose and she lay bereft of purpose. Tears and sobbing made her a sight one wearied of seeing. Without fine clothes or ornaments, her beauty had faded.

Today she appeared as a radiant beauty, a well-formed painting, a colourful peacock, and a fragrant flower. Who would not have doubted?

He later recognized that she was indeed his daughter. But why did the woman whose beauty had faded while she lived with him appear beautiful here? Why did the woman once filled with tears and sobbing now appear filled with joy?

This question first made him think. After he understood that he himself had caused her former condition, waves of thought rose within him. He hastened to discover where they led. At last, the central question was this:

Life is surrounded by countless sorrows. Among them, how great a sorrow it is when a man and woman whose hearts do not unite must live together. A life of happiness is the fruit of united hearts. Is that not what we see here?

Should we support these people who delight in such a happy life? Or should we kill them because they scorned scripture, crossed society's restrictions, cast aside the barrier of caste, and brought disgrace upon the lineage?

He had to find an answer. He thought for a while. The townspeople's abuse stood on one side and the love of two united hearts on the other. Both tormented him.

"Shame! Our intention is cruel. They are attaining the true benefit of human birth. To destroy them would be crueller than cruelty itself.

"We gave birth to her and raised her, and then plunged her into great sorrow. Only now do we understand that such suffering is unnatural. She who struggled in an ocean of sorrow now leaps and plays in an ocean of joy.

"This makes clear that love has no caste and that social restraint cannot stop it. To make young girls widows and ruin their lives is a great sin. If God himself could approve widowhood, why did he give them the feeling of love?

"This is society's ignorance. Society considers it shameful. Must two lives be sacrificed to erase that shame? No, no!"

He reached this conclusion. His daughter's life filled with affection and passionate love gave him inward joy. Yet the abuse of ignorant townspeople and the restraints of caste appeared to torment him.

Weariness overcame him. He leaned weakly against the wall, unaware that the billhook had fallen.

When the lovers whom society regarded as criminals later came before him, bowed, and asked for punishment, his heart throbbed. Sorrow filled his breast.

"Are we to become Yama to these people and their pure life? Shame! What could be more cruel? Even if society prevents us from delighting in their happy life, we must not prevent them from living it," he decided.

Without saying anything, he walked quickly home.

When Amuthan and Kokilam understood this, was there any limit to their happiness? His compassionate heart caused love to grow and happiness to flourish in their lives. How could it be a crime that these two people of good character gained a married life?

A short story written by Anna under the pen name Samadharman. *Dravida Nadu*, 25 July 1943.
